ows you  to  correlate your data across multiple  slides  across multiple studies and across  multiple imagers should that be the case  all of the image processing and  alignment is  automated and refined and this is really  what enables  the downstream single cell analysis  the software is coupled with the imager  the imager is built on our in-cell  platform which  released in 2001 we are experts in  precision engineering and this system is  fast precise  and sensitive we made some improvements  to  um better facilitate  tissue imaging so the system is capable  of imaging  in five channels or wavelengths at the  same time  the barcode reader is integrated with  the system and with the software so this  is how you can track your slides  it's compatible with a slide handling  robot so  you can walk away while it does the  imaging the image processing and image  alignment  it has a large maximum imaging area  it can handle very large tissue  with absolutely no issues  the final component of the cell dive  multiplex imaging system is halo image  analysis software and we  chose halo for its ease of use and  scalability  the interface is very logical it's very  intuitive and what we really wanted to  do  is to bring the capability to analyze  these images  to people who may not have any  bioinformatics experience  we've worked with halo extensively  during our beta testing period  and we found their responsiveness and  user support to be  world class halo comes in different  modules with different licenses so you  can choose  what best suits your work  so here's just one example of how all of  these come together  all of these components come together to  provide the capability to do true single  cell analysis  so what you see here on the left is the  image of a tonsil tissue  that was part of a 30 marker cell dive  study here we've highlighted five of  those markers and as you progress from  left to right you can see  staining segmentation classification  and finally down to single cell analysis  of cellular phenotypes  this is just one of many examples of how  the cell dive imaging system  in all of its components comes together  to  bring you reliable reproducible results  none of this would have been possible  without the expertise  the innovation and the sheer persistence  of the ge research cell dive team  past and present we owe them a huge  thank you  without them we wouldn't be bringing  this capability to the market today  so we'd love to keep in touch with you  if you'd like more information  you can go to saitiva.com forward slash  cell dive  or drop us a note dot die  at syteva.com  thank you for your time and attention  today and um  we'd be happy to take questions  ask please do so now just click on the  ask a question box located in the far  left of your screen  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  so let's get started  uh first of all i'd like to ask a doctor  ginty  does the order of antibody staining  affect the results  if i use an antibody in an earlier or  later staining cycle  does it make a difference  get here  um  thank you paul sorry we've had a couple  of uh  technical issues here with our mute  buttons i think that's  par for the course these days um yes the  um we do when when we uh validate our  antibody workflow there's a few things  to consider  in the um selection of position in the  multiplexing sequence  um there's an option to do primary  secondary uh staining in the very first  round so that's always a nice  uh option um if you want to do if you  don't have to go through the conjugation  process  and you can include your primary  secondaries in the first round  and there's also a second consideration  whether or not the antigen  is affected by the inactivation process  and that's part of our antibody  validation workflow that we test  the epitope and the staining the  sensitivity um  post uh sort of a pseudo  inactivation process and now if that  epitope is affected and we found about  10  of epitopes are and that epidural should  also be  our antibodies should also be placed up  front  and otherwise um what we've done over  the years is  singleplex versus multiplex and we have  not found the difference between  uh epitopes that are or staining  intensity for  for antibodies that are seen as part of  a multiplex sequence versus single flex  um i think that that's you know um  something that everyone should consider  as they design their  workflows and to compare their  individual antibodies and  back to singleplex staining um  but the two important considerations are  whether or not you want to do a primary  secondary  in your first round or uh  thank you very much uh this is a  question for  um for prachi  how long does it take to do a cycle of  imaging and staining  yeah that's a great question it's going  to really depend on how many markers you  want to do and how big  your imaging area or area of interest is  but if we take the example let's say you  want to do a whole slide  and you wanted to do 10 rounds of  multiplexing so that's  40 different biomarkers your time is  about  50 hours that's for staining and imaging  please keep in mind that with the robot  you can  image overnight so the hands-on time is  significantly lower than that  thank you dr genty uh  you have a question of is it possible to  combine multiplex  imaging of protein and rna for a more  comprehensive view of the cells or  tissues being studied  and it certainly is possible the only  consideration to bear in mind is  protease effects on your um on your  proteins so  what we would recommend is to uh test  that  pre the preparation protocol together  with the effects on your um your  proteins and some of our colleagues uh  former  colleagues at neogenomics have recently  shown the acd  combination of acd rna scope together  with  protein multiplexing in one of their  posters um so i think  it's possible but be aware of the  protein's effects on  on on your um on your proteins  great thank you uh i'm going to come  back to you again fiona on this one uh  people are asking the question is it  possible you describe the process  as being direct conjugated antibodies is  it possible to use secondaries or other  forms of amplification  to identify the presence of low  abundance proteins  yeah as i said in in my presentation in  a  few minutes ago the primary secondary  option is certainly available  for the first round of staining for  second round depending on how many  species  you have you want to obviously avoid  that cross reactivity  that is occurs with primary secondary  staining and that's  one of the reasons why direct  conjugation alleviates or works around  that issue  um so i think yes the answer is you can  use primary secondary staining  and then direct conjugation is then a  requirement obviously for your  for your later rounds  great um sorry to keep picking on you  fiona but a lot of people have questions  for your presentation  uh can you please comment on the  reproducibility of the assay  so if i were to repeat the assay  obviously you can't on that  probably can't on the same section but  in the same tumor  how reproducible is it yeah we've done  quite a number of different experiments  to evaluate that  pole so we've looked at sequential  sections we've looked at the correlation  between markers between sections granted  there is going to be slightly slight  variability which can be  in the order of depending on how  abundant your biomarker is you could see  a variation of maybe um from slide to  slide  maybe 10 to 20 variability anyway  because of differences in numbers of  slides of where you've cut into the  section  but overall uh we see a very high degree  of repeatability  um and that's helped for sure by the  calibration process  and the correction process that we use  consistently  for every study we do  great i'm getting several questions here  fiona about  the the principle behind the  inactivation of the die would you care  to comment on that  so the um when we looked at the  different methods of inactivation we  evaluated many different types  back in 2006 including stripping uh acid  um we've actually settled on  um an oxidation a mild oxidation process  knowing that cyanine dies have that  vulnerability  and really leveraged that as well as  alexa dies  and so we we are one of our scientists  and at the time  understanding the vulnerabilities of  these dyes took that to  our advantage in terms of the oxidation  process so the uh  we developed that we tested it over many  different  um um permutations  um ph is very important uh hydrogen  peroxide basic it's it's  a formulation that we um have uh  described in our publications as well as  is included  within the protocols um provided to  customers  and um i think the key thing is it was  sort of taking an advantage of a  weakness  within the diet that was um in terms of  its vulnerability to oxidation so the  thymine dies and the electrodives  uh fall within that criteria not every  dye is oxidizable um  in this in this regard so the fitzy for  example um  less so um although very light sensitive  and um yeah so hopefully that answers  that question  yeah thank you thank you approach a  question for you we're being asked about  the automation of the workflow what does  that look like  and how is that performed right so  um very briefly there are two  options to stain the slides depending on  your throughput and the level of  automation you'd like to have  the openwell cassette option  is compatible with pipetting robots  so you could automate the staining i  think most easily that way there are  other options with coverslips which  you can also set up but a little bit  chat a little more challenging a little  more work little more cost  so you can use a pipetting robot with  the open well  um cassette but the best use of  time that we've seen with our  collaborators is to do automated imaging  overnight  with a plate handling robot so the  slides image while you sleep  and you come back to the images in the  morning and that tends to be  the best use of of uh automation  is using it in that downtime  um and also for you apache um  is there an optimized protocol for  conjugate or primary conjugation  uh of the antibodies yes  okay yes okay yes so with the  the cell dive solution comes the  complete list  of validated antibodies all the  information on sourcing  what the best delusions are conjugation  protocols step by step  walking through how to get the best  results with the standing  there's a question about halo approach  i'm going to leave this to you  um can can halo do segmentation  uh better than just doing a ring around  the nucleus  so is it possible to identify the  cytoplasm is not just a nuclear state  absolutely yes so i will  defer more detailed questions on halo 2  indica labs who are prepared to work  with  our cell dive users but  yes certainly you can do better  segmentation than that  great and uh also for your project in  the uh  commercial implementation what are what  objective do you use  usually use for the imaging uh of the  sample  you typically use a 20x  yeah do you know dna numerical aperture  i do and now i'm going to get myself  confused with the coverslip and  non-coverslip  0.45 yeah okay thank you  uh so uh thank you very much um uh  we're about out of time for questions so  i think we need to move along but  um uh any final comments you might have  fiona or prachee  thank you for your time yeah uh  so thank you for your time today and  your important research we'd also like  to thank lab roots our spa and our  and saitiva for underwriting today's  educational  webcast before we go i'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today  they're interesting questions  questions that we did not have time to  answer  but we're submitted on demand um we'll  be addressing  and we will get back to you by email i  would like to again thank  dr